1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,080
In 2007, a phone company in Kenya
launched a way to send money by text

2
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:11,360
message. It hoped three hundred and fifty
thousand people would sign up in the first

3
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:14,960
year. One point two million did.

4
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:20,640
Today, that service handles about one hundred
and twenty-seven million transactions.

5
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:24,560
Every single day. Three years after Kenya,

6
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:28,560
the same idea came to South
Africa, with a big target:

7
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:33,840
ten million users. Six years
later, it was switched off.

8
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:36,640
This is the story of M-Pesa.

9
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:40,293
Why it took over Kenya,
why it failed here,

10
00:00:40,347 --> 00:00:44,400
and what it tells us about how
South Africans really move money.

11
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:49,600
To understand M-Pesa,
you have to picture Kenya in 2006.

12
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:52,773
Millions of people had moved
to the cities for work,

13
00:00:52,827 --> 00:00:56,240
while their families stayed
behind in rural villages.

14
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:59,573
Every month, they needed
to send money home.

15
00:00:59,627 --> 00:01:03,520
But only about nineteen percent
of Kenyans had a bank account.

16
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:07,653
So many people sent cash
with bus drivers or friends,

17
00:01:07,707 --> 00:01:09,720
or carried it home themselves.

18
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:12,360
It was slow, and it was risky.

19
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:15,600
Money got lost. Money got stolen.

20
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:21,320
But almost everyone had something else
in their pocket: a basic mobile phone.

21
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:25,173
M-Pesa didn't even start as
a money transfer service.

22
00:01:25,227 --> 00:01:29,640
In 2005, Safaricom,
Kenya's biggest mobile network,

23
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:34,000
ran a small pilot with Vodafone
and a microfinance lender.

24
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:39,093
Around five hundred people used their
phones to receive and repay small loans,

25
00:01:39,147 --> 00:01:41,160
through just eight agents.

26
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:44,507
Then the team noticed
something unexpected.

27
00:01:44,613 --> 00:01:47,720
People were using it to send
money to their relatives.

28
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:49,973
So they changed the plan.

29
00:01:50,027 --> 00:01:55,240
The main idea became three
simple words: send money home.

30
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:58,507
M-Pesa launched in March 2007.

31
00:01:58,533 --> 00:02:01,173
"Pesa" means money in Swahili.

32
00:02:01,227 --> 00:02:04,453
You gave cash to an agent,
usually a small shop,

33
00:02:04,507 --> 00:02:07,560
and the money appeared as
a balance on your phone.

34
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:09,880
You could send it to anyone with a number,

35
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:14,533
and they could cash it out at another
agent, hundreds of kilometres away.

36
00:02:14,587 --> 00:02:18,520
It ran on the simplest phones,
with no internet needed.

37
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:24,160
And it exploded. So why did
it work so well in Kenya?

38
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:29,600
First, one network. Safaricom had
about eighty percent of Kenya's mobile

39
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:33,813
users. When almost everyone you
know is on the same network,

40
00:02:33,867 --> 00:02:36,680
a service like this spreads fast.

41
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:39,760
Second, the regulator let it grow.

42
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:41,440
Not everyone liked it.

43
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:45,840
One former central bank governor later
said some officials called it a money

44
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:48,760
laundering tool, or a pyramid scheme.

45
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:54,613
But Kenya's central bank gave it a letter
of no objection, then watched it closely.

46
00:02:54,667 --> 00:02:58,920
In 2008, the finance minister
even ordered an audit.

47
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:02,960
M-Pesa passed. Third, agents everywhere.

48
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:04,640
In just one year,

49
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:09,200
the number of agents grew from about two
thousand two hundred to more than eight

50
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:11,040
thousand six hundred.

51
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:13,760
And fourth, a real need.

52
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:16,080
People didn't need to be convinced.

53
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:19,440
They were already sending
money home, the hard way.

54
00:03:20,322 --> 00:03:24,642
Fast forward to today, and the
numbers are hard to believe.

55
00:03:24,722 --> 00:03:27,202
In the year to March 2026,

56
00:03:27,282 --> 00:03:32,162
M-Pesa had about forty-one million
active customers in a month.

57
00:03:32,242 --> 00:03:36,242
It handled forty-six billion
transactions in a year.

58
00:03:36,322 --> 00:03:39,922
That's around one hundred and
twenty-seven million a day,

59
00:03:40,002 --> 00:03:43,362
worth almost forty-two
trillion Kenyan shillings,

60
00:03:43,482 --> 00:03:47,122
through more than three hundred
and thirty thousand agents.

61
00:03:47,202 --> 00:03:52,482
In 2011, about forty-two percent of
Kenyan adults had a bank or mobile money

62
00:03:52,562 --> 00:03:57,202
account. By 2024, it was ninety percent.

63
00:03:57,282 --> 00:04:02,402
So in 2010, the obvious question
was: if it worked in Kenya,

64
00:04:02,482 --> 00:04:03,962
why not South Africa?

65
00:04:04,402 --> 00:04:10,002
In August 2010, Vodacom brought M-Pesa
to South Africa, together with Nedbank.

66
00:04:10,562 --> 00:04:14,362
The target was ten million
users within three years.

67
00:04:14,722 --> 00:04:16,802
But South Africa had a different rule.

68
00:04:17,282 --> 00:04:22,482
The Reserve Bank had said that only
registered banks may issue electronic money.

69
00:04:22,602 --> 00:04:26,402
So M-Pesa couldn't simply be
a phone company product here.

70
00:04:26,482 --> 00:04:29,042
It had to work through a bank partner.

71
00:04:29,122 --> 00:04:34,962
And to sign up, you needed your ID or
passport, and a RICA-registered SIM card.

72
00:04:35,042 --> 00:04:40,802
By May 2011, it had just over one
hundred thousand registered users.

73
00:04:40,962 --> 00:04:42,882
Vodacom didn't give up.

74
00:04:42,962 --> 00:04:48,482
In 2014, it relaunched M-Pesa with
a new bank partner, Bidvest Bank,

75
00:04:48,562 --> 00:04:52,322
a Visa card, and more than
eight thousand agents.

76
00:04:52,442 --> 00:04:55,922
Its CEO, Shameel Joosub, put it simply:

77
00:04:56,002 --> 00:05:00,322
if you need to take a taxi to
use the service, it will fail.

78
00:05:00,402 --> 00:05:02,402
It still didn't take off.

79
00:05:02,482 --> 00:05:07,575
In the year to March 2015, about
one million people had registered.

80
00:05:07,629 --> 00:05:12,202
But only around forty-two thousand
had used it in the past month.

81
00:05:12,242 --> 00:05:18,122
In May 2016, Vodacom announced it was
shutting M-Pesa down in South Africa.

82
00:05:18,162 --> 00:05:23,642
Its words: there was little prospect
of reaching a critical mass of users.

83
00:05:23,682 --> 00:05:28,882
M-Pesa was gone. But the need to send
money and support home never went

84
00:05:28,962 --> 00:05:33,362
away. It's exactly the problem
we work on at Fluxr.

85
00:05:33,442 --> 00:05:36,162
This story is brought to you by Fluxr.

86
