91
00:05:56,829 --> 00:05:58,722
Now, back to the story.

92
00:05:58,802 --> 00:06:01,482
So why did M-Pesa fail here?

93
00:06:01,522 --> 00:06:04,482
Analysts point to a few big reasons.

94
00:06:04,562 --> 00:06:08,202
One. More South Africans
were already banked.

95
00:06:08,242 --> 00:06:13,202
In 2010, a national survey found that
sixty-three percent of adults had a bank

96
00:06:13,642 --> 00:06:17,202
account. In Kenya, it was far fewer.

97
00:06:17,282 --> 00:06:19,682
Two. Strict sign-up rules.

98
00:06:19,762 --> 00:06:24,242
Opening a mobile money account here
needed much the same paperwork as a bank

99
00:06:24,322 --> 00:06:28,562
account. That made it harder
for many people to sign up.

100
00:06:28,762 --> 00:06:31,522
Three. Too few agents.

101
00:06:31,642 --> 00:06:35,362
South Africa had about eight
thousand M-Pesa agents.

102
00:06:35,442 --> 00:06:38,402
Kenya had more than sixty thousand.

103
00:06:38,482 --> 00:06:41,122
Four. Tough competition.

104
00:06:41,202 --> 00:06:45,042
The banks and the big retailers
were already moving money.

105
00:06:45,122 --> 00:06:50,962
As one tech analyst said: you could create
demand, but there wasn't a pressing need.

106
00:06:51,082 --> 00:06:54,802
And five, no single dominant network.

107
00:06:54,882 --> 00:06:58,322
In Kenya, one network had almost everyone.

108
00:06:58,402 --> 00:07:01,682
In South Africa, people
were spread across many.

109
00:07:02,553 --> 00:07:05,673
So how did South Africans
move money instead?

110
00:07:05,993 --> 00:07:07,713
The banks moved first.

111
00:07:07,993 --> 00:07:11,193
Absa launched CashSend in 2008,

112
00:07:11,273 --> 00:07:14,633
so you could send money to
someone without a bank account.

113
00:07:14,713 --> 00:07:18,073
FNB launched eWallet in 2009.

114
00:07:18,153 --> 00:07:21,593
Standard Bank launched
Instant Money in 2010.

115
00:07:21,673 --> 00:07:27,193
Many of these work through USSD:
those star and hash codes you dial.

116
00:07:27,273 --> 00:07:29,273
Then the shops joined in.

117
00:07:29,513 --> 00:07:33,993
Today you can send money at a till,
for under ten rand a transfer.

118
00:07:34,113 --> 00:07:36,446
And then came cash vouchers.

119
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:39,113
You pay cash at a shop and get a PIN.

120
00:07:39,193 --> 00:07:45,193
1Voucher, OTT Voucher and Blu Voucher are
now sold at thousands of stores across

121
00:07:45,273 --> 00:07:50,100
the country. And underneath
a lot of it sits USSD.

122
00:07:50,126 --> 00:07:52,833
It's a phone standard from
the nineteen nineties.

123
00:07:52,873 --> 00:07:56,393
It runs on any phone,
and it doesn't need data.

124
00:07:56,473 --> 00:08:01,593
The ITU estimates that over ninety percent
of mobile money transactions in Africa

125
00:08:01,673 --> 00:08:05,233
run on USSD. In 2024,

126
00:08:05,273 --> 00:08:09,953
only about a quarter of people in
Sub-Saharan Africa used mobile internet.

127
00:08:09,993 --> 00:08:12,846
So the codes you dial still matter.

128
00:08:12,900 --> 00:08:16,993
There's one more part of the story,
and it hits close to home.

129
00:08:17,033 --> 00:08:18,673
According to the World Bank,

130
00:08:18,713 --> 00:08:23,633
South Africa is the most expensive
G20 country to send money from.

131
00:08:23,673 --> 00:08:25,553
At the end of 2024,

132
00:08:25,593 --> 00:08:30,553
sending two hundred US dollars from
South Africa cost about eleven percent on

133
00:08:31,033 --> 00:08:34,433
average. The global average
was about six percent.

134
00:08:34,473 --> 00:08:37,193
And there are a lot of people sending.

135
00:08:37,233 --> 00:08:42,793
The 2022 census counted about two point
four million people in South Africa who were

136
00:08:42,953 --> 00:08:47,393
born in another country, including
about one million born in Zimbabwe,

137
00:08:47,433 --> 00:08:52,193
four hundred thousand in Mozambique,
and two hundred thousand in Malawi.

138
00:08:52,233 --> 00:08:55,033
So here's the lesson of M-Pesa.

139
00:08:55,193 --> 00:08:57,473
Great technology isn't enough.

140
00:08:57,513 --> 00:09:00,673
It has to fit how people already live.

141
00:09:00,713 --> 00:09:04,273
In Kenya, that meant
agents and one network.

142
00:09:04,313 --> 00:09:07,406
In South Africa, it means cash, vouchers,

143
00:09:07,460 --> 00:09:10,353
and a code you can dial from any phone.

144
00:09:10,393 --> 00:09:14,606
Let's recap. M-Pesa started
as a small loan pilot,

145
00:09:14,660 --> 00:09:17,633
and became Kenya's way
of sending money home.

146
00:09:17,673 --> 00:09:22,673
It worked because of one dominant
network, a regulator that let it grow,

147
00:09:22,713 --> 00:09:26,046
agents everywhere, and a real need.

148
00:09:26,100 --> 00:09:31,593
In South Africa, it met a banked
population, strict rules, too few agents,

149
00:09:31,673 --> 00:09:36,126
and strong competition,
and it shut down in 2016.

150
00:09:36,180 --> 00:09:39,473
If you enjoyed this story,
subscribe for the next one.

151
00:09:39,513 --> 00:09:43,673
And if you're sending airtime and
data home, you know where to find us.

